# Physics 11 - written explanations (round 3)
@phys11-u1-q01
Q: Light passes from air into glass at i = 30°; n(glass) = 1.5. Find the angle of refraction r.
W: Snell's law: n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r. | 1.0 × sin 30° = 1.5 × sin r → 0.5 = 1.5 sin r. | sin r = 1/3 = 0.333, so r ≈ 19.5° (bent toward the normal).
T: Into a denser medium (larger n), the ray bends toward the normal.
@phys11-u1-q02
W: Refractive index n = c/v. | n = (3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (2.0 × 10⁸ m/s). | n = 1.5 (no unit).
T: n is always ≥ 1 because nothing travels faster than c.
@phys11-u1-q03
W: n = c/v, so v = c/n. | v = (3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ 1.5. | v = 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s.
T: Larger n → slower light.
@phys11-u1-q04
W: For glass → air: sin C = n₂/n₁ = 1/n. | sin C = 1/1.5 = 0.667. | C = sin⁻¹(0.667) ≈ 41.8°.
T: Critical angle only exists going from the denser to the less dense medium.
@phys11-u1-q05
Q: A ray in glass (n = 1.5) meets the glass–air surface at i = 50°. Is there total internal reflection?
W: Critical angle: sin C = 1/1.5 → C ≈ 41.8°. | Compare: i = 50° > C. | So total internal reflection occurs – no ray emerges into the air.
T: TIR needs (1) dense → less dense and (2) i > C.
@phys11-u1-q06
Q: A ray in glass (n = 1.5) meets the glass–air surface at i = 20°. Is there total internal reflection?
W: C ≈ 41.8° for glass (n = 1.5). | i = 20° < C. | No TIR: the ray refracts out, bending away from the normal (sin r = 1.5 sin 20° = 0.513, r ≈ 31°).
T: Below C the ray escapes (with a weak reflection too).
@phys11-u1-q07
W: Light from the pool floor bends away from the normal as it leaves the water. | Your eye traces the rays back in straight lines. | They appear to come from a point higher than the real floor – a raised virtual image, so the pool looks shallower.
T: Apparent depth = real depth ÷ n (≈ ¾ of the real depth for water).
@phys11-u1-q08
W: Without water, the rim blocks the straight path from the coin to your eye. | Water makes light from the coin bend away from the normal at the surface. | The bent rays now pass over the rim into your eye, so the coin appears.
T: Refraction lets you "see round" an edge.
@phys11-u1-q11
Q: A convex lens has f = 10 cm and an object is 30 cm away (real-is-positive). Find the image distance v.
W: Lens formula: 1/f = 1/u + 1/v → 1/v = 1/f − 1/u. | 1/v = 1/10 − 1/30 = 3/30 − 1/30 = 2/30 = 1/15. | v = +15 cm: a real image on the other side of the lens.
T: Object beyond 2F (20 cm) → image between F and 2F, real and smaller.
@phys11-u1-q12
Q: Find the magnification when v = 15 cm and u = 30 cm.
W: Magnification |m| = v/u (image height ÷ object height). | |m| = 15/30. | |m| = 0.5 – the image is half size, inverted and real.
T: |m| < 1 means diminished.
@phys11-u1-q13
W: Object at 2F: u = 2f. | 1/v = 1/f − 1/(2f) = 1/(2f), so v = 2f. | m = v/u = 1: same size, inverted, real, at 2F on the other side.
T: This is how a photocopier makes a same-size copy.
@phys11-u1-q15
W: Object inside F: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u is negative because 1/u > 1/f. | Negative v means the image is virtual, on the same side as the object. | The rays diverge after the lens, and the image is upright and larger – a magnifying glass.
T: Magnifying glass: object closer than f.
@phys11-u1-q16
W: A concave lens spreads (diverges) parallel rays. | The refracted rays only appear to come from a point on the object's side. | So the image is always virtual, upright and smaller, between the lens and F.
T: Concave lens: always virtual, erect, diminished.
@phys11-u1-q17
W: Power P = 1/f with f in metres. | f = 20 cm = 0.20 m. | P = 1/0.20 = +5 D (converging).
T: Change cm to m before using P = 1/f.
@phys11-u1-q18
W: P = 1/f with f in metres: f = −25 cm = −0.25 m. | P = 1/(−0.25). | P = −4 D – negative, so a diverging (concave) lens.
T: Positive power = converging; negative power = diverging.
@phys11-u1-q19
W: Thin lenses in contact: powers add, P = P₁ + P₂. | P = 2 D + 3 D = 5 D. | f = 1/P = 1/5 = 0.20 m (20 cm).
T: Add powers, not focal lengths.
@phys11-u1-q20
W: The refractive index of glass is slightly different for each colour. | Violet has the largest n, so it bends most; red bends least. | The colours spread out into a spectrum – dispersion.
T: ROYGBIV: red deviates least, violet most.
@phys11-u1-q22
W: sin C = 1/n. | sin C = 1/√3 = 0.577. | C = sin⁻¹(0.577) ≈ 35.3°.
T: Larger n → smaller critical angle.
@phys11-u1-q23
W: Normal incidence means the ray travels along the normal (i = 0°). | Snell's law: sin r = sin 0°/n = 0, so r = 0°. | The ray does not bend; only its speed (and wavelength) changes.
T: Along the normal: no bending.
@phys11-u1-q24
W: Diamond has a very high refractive index (n ≈ 2.42). | So its critical angle is small: sin C = 1/2.42 → C ≈ 24°. | Light inside a cut diamond meets surfaces at angles > C many times, so it is totally internally reflected and comes out of the top, sparkling.
T: High n → small C → lots of TIR.
@phys11-u1-q25
W: A real image forms where light rays actually meet. | A virtual image is where rays only appear to come from when traced backwards. | A real image can be caught on a screen; a virtual image cannot.
T: Real = screen; virtual = look into the lens/mirror.
@phys11-u1-q27
W: The size of m gives the size ratio: |m| = 2, so the image is twice as tall. | The negative sign (school convention) means the image is inverted. | An inverted image from one lens is real.
T: Sign of m = orientation; size of m = enlargement.
@phys11-u1-q28
W: Water has a larger refractive index than air (1.33 > 1.00). | Light slows down entering water. | So the ray bends toward the normal.
T: Slower medium → toward the normal.
@phys11-u1-q29
W: Each colour (wavelength) travels at a slightly different speed in glass or water. | So n is slightly different for each colour (larger for violet). | This variation of n with wavelength is called dispersion.
T: Dispersion causes rainbows and prism spectra.
@phys11-u1-q30
W: For a far object, the eye muscles relax and the lens is thinnest (longest f), so the image falls on the retina. | In short sight, the eyeball is too long (or the lens too strong), so the image of a far object forms in front of the retina. | A diverging (concave) spectacle lens spreads the rays so the image moves back onto the retina.
T: Short sight → concave (negative) lens; long sight → convex (positive) lens.
@phys11-u1-q31
Q: Light goes from air into glass (n = 1.50) at i = 28°. Find the angle of refraction r.
W: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ with n₁ = 1.00, θ₁ = 28°, n₂ = 1.50. | sin θ₂ = sin 28° / 1.50 = 0.469/1.50 = 0.313. | θ₂ = sin⁻¹(0.313) ≈ 18.2°, toward the normal.
T: Check: r < i when entering a denser medium.
@phys11-u1-q32
Q: Light goes from air into water (n = 1.33) at i = 40°. Find the angle of refraction r.
W: sin r = (n₁/n₂) sin i = sin 40° / 1.33. | sin r = 0.643/1.33 = 0.483. | r = sin⁻¹(0.483) ≈ 28.9°.
T: Keep 3 significant figures in the sine before taking sin⁻¹.
@phys11-u1-q33
Q: In glass (n = 1.52) the refracted ray makes 20° with the normal after entering from air. Find the angle of incidence i.
W: n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r with n₁ = 1.00. | sin i = 1.52 × sin 20° = 1.52 × 0.342 = 0.520. | i = sin⁻¹(0.520) ≈ 31.3°.
T: The angle in air is always the larger one.
@phys11-u1-q35
Q: A material has n = 1.40. What is the speed of light in it?
W: v = c/n. | v = (3.00 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ 1.40. | v ≈ 2.14 × 10⁸ m/s.
T: The answer must be less than 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.
@phys11-u1-q37
Q: A ray in water (n = 1.33) meets the water–air surface at i = 45°. Is there total internal reflection?
W: Critical angle: sin C = 1/1.33 = 0.752, C ≈ 48.8°. | Compare: 45° < 48.8°. | No TIR – the ray refracts out into the air (close to the surface).
T: Always find C first, then compare it with i.
@phys11-u1-q38
Q: A ray in water (n = 1.33) meets the water–air surface at i = 55°. Is there total internal reflection?
W: C ≈ 48.8° for water → air. | 55° > 48.8°, so TIR occurs. | The ray reflects back into the water with angle of reflection = 55°.
T: In TIR the law of reflection still applies: i = r.
@phys11-u1-q39
Q: Find the critical angle for light going from glass (n = 1.50) into water (n = 1.33).
W: For a denser → less dense boundary: sin C = n₂/n₁. | sin C = 1.33/1.50 = 0.887. | C = sin⁻¹(0.887) ≈ 62.5°.
T: With water instead of air outside, C is larger.
@phys11-u1-q40
Q: A ray in a medium of n = 1.8 meets a medium of n = 1.2 at i = 40°. Find the angle of refraction.
W: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂: 1.8 × sin 40° = 1.2 × sin r. | sin r = 1.8 × 0.643 / 1.2 = 0.964. | r = sin⁻¹(0.964) ≈ 74.6°, bent away from the normal.
T: If the sine came out above 1, it would mean TIR.
@phys11-u1-q42
W: Light from the fish bends away from the normal as it leaves the water. | You see a virtual image of the fish higher (shallower) than the real fish. | So the spear must be aimed below the image, at the real fish.
T: Objects under water are deeper than they look.
@phys11-u1-q43
W: Apparent depth h' = real depth ÷ n. | h' = 4.00 m ÷ 1.33. | h' ≈ 3.01 m.
T: Apparent depth is always less than the real depth.
@phys11-u1-q45
W: Glass has a slightly larger n for violet than for red. | So at each face violet bends more than red. | White light spreads into a spectrum, with red deviated least and violet most.
T: Newton recombined the spectrum with a second prism to prove white light is a mixture.
@phys11-u1-q47
Q: A convex lens has f = +15 cm and the object is 45 cm away (real-is-positive). Find v.
W: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u. | 1/v = 1/15 − 1/45 = 3/45 − 1/45 = 2/45. | v = 45/2 = 22.5 cm – positive, so real.
T: Use a common denominator to avoid rounding errors.
@phys11-u1-q48
W: |m| = v/u. | |m| = 22.5 cm ÷ 45 cm. | |m| = 0.50 – the image is real, inverted and half size.
T: Object beyond 2F → diminished image.
@phys11-u1-q49
Q: A convex lens has f = +15 cm and the object is 10 cm away. Find v and describe the image.
W: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/15 − 1/10. | = 2/30 − 3/30 = −1/30, so v = −30 cm. | Negative v: virtual, upright, magnified (m = 30/10 = 3).
T: Object inside f of a convex lens → magnifying glass.
@phys11-u1-q50
Q: A concave lens has f = −20 cm and the object is 20 cm away. Find v and describe the image.
W: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/20 − 1/20. | = −2/20 = −1/10, so v = −10 cm. | Virtual, upright, diminished (m = 10/20 = 0.5).
T: Concave lens: f is negative in real-is-positive.
@phys11-u1-q53
W: Powers in contact add: P = +3.0 D + (−1.0 D). | P = +2.0 D. | f = 1/P = 1/2.0 = +0.50 m (converging).
T: Keep the sign of each power.
@phys11-u1-q55
W: Object at 2F: u = 2f gives v = 2f (from 1/v = 1/f − 1/2f = 1/2f). | m = v/u = 1. | Image: real, inverted, same size, at 2F on the far side.
T: 2F in → 2F out.
@phys11-u1-q56
W: Object between F and the lens: u < f. | 1/v = 1/f − 1/u < 0, so v is negative – virtual. | The image is upright, enlarged and on the same side as the object.
T: Magnifying glass position.
@phys11-u1-q57
W: A concave lens diverges light for every object position. | Refracted rays appear to come from a point between F and the lens. | The image is always virtual, upright, diminished, on the object's side.
T: Concave lenses never form real images of real objects.
@phys11-u1-q58
Q: An object and its real image are both 30 cm from a convex lens (u = v = 30 cm). Find f.
W: 1/f = 1/u + 1/v. | 1/f = 1/30 + 1/30 = 2/30 = 1/15. | f = 15 cm – this is the "object at 2F" case (2f = 30 cm).
T: u = v means the object is at 2F.
@phys11-u1-q59
Q: A convex lens forms a real image with magnification m = −3 when u = 12 cm. Find v and f.
W: |m| = v/u, so v = 3 × 12 = 36 cm (on the other side). | 1/f = 1/u + 1/v = 1/12 + 1/36 = 3/36 + 1/36 = 4/36 = 1/9. | f = 9 cm.
T: Find v from m first, then use the lens formula.
@phys11-u1-q60
Q: In an eye defect, the image of a distant object forms in front of the retina. Name the defect and the lens needed.
W: Image in front of the retina for far objects = short sight (myopia). | The eye converges light too much. | A diverging (concave) lens with negative power spreads the rays so the image moves back onto the retina.
T: Myopia: Minus lens.
@phys11-u1-q61
Q: What kind of spectacle lens does long sight (hyperopia) need?
W: In long sight, near objects form images behind the retina. | The eye does not converge light enough. | A converging (convex) lens with positive power adds the missing convergence.
T: Hyperopia: plus (convex) lens.
@phys11-u1-q63
W: Two prisms placed apex-to-apex (thin in the middle, thick at the edges) both bend light toward their bases. | The bases point outward, so rays bend away from the axis. | That is how a concave (diverging) lens works.
T: Base-to-base prisms model a convex (converging) lens.
@phys11-u1-q64
W: n is slightly different for each wavelength (colour). | So different colours refract by different amounts. | This effect is called dispersion.
T: Dispersion = splitting white light into colours.
@phys11-u1-q65
Q: A ray travels along the normal from air into glass. What is its deviation?
W: i = 0°, so sin r = 0 and r = 0°. | The ray does not change direction. | Deviation = 0°; only the speed (and wavelength) changes.
T: Along the normal, no bending at any boundary.
@phys11-u1-q68
Q: A ray passes through a glass slab with parallel faces. What is its net deviation?
W: At the first face it bends toward the normal; at the second face it bends away by the same amount. | Because the faces are parallel, the emerging ray is parallel to the incident ray. | Net deviation = 0, but the ray is shifted sideways (lateral displacement).
T: Thicker slab or larger angle → bigger sideways shift.
@phys11-u1-q70
W: At the air–glass surface: sin r = sin 30°/1.5 = 0.333, so r ≈ 19.5°. | The ray then meets the mirror along the mirror's normal (i = 0° at the mirror). | It reflects straight back on itself and retraces its path out of the glass.
T: A ray along a mirror's normal returns along the same line.
@phys11-u1-q71
W: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ with n₁ = 1.00, θ₁ = 35°, n₂ = 1.50. | sin θ₂ = sin 35° / 1.50 = 0.574/1.50 = 0.382. | θ₂ = sin⁻¹(0.382) ≈ 22.4°, toward the normal.
T: Write the given values in a list before substituting.
@phys11-u1-q72
W: n = c/v. | n = (3.00 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (1.24 × 10⁸ m/s). | n ≈ 2.42 (this is diamond).
T: Speeds cancel units – n has no unit.
@phys11-u1-q73
W: v = c/n. | v = (3.00 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ 1.33. | v ≈ 2.26 × 10⁸ m/s.
T: Light in water is about ¾ of c.
@phys11-u1-q74
Q: Find the critical angle for water (n = 1.33) to air.
W: sin C = 1/n. | sin C = 1/1.33 = 0.752. | C = sin⁻¹(0.752) ≈ 48.8°.
T: Remember 48.8° for water and about 42° for glass.
@phys11-u1-q75
Q: A ray in water (n = 1.33) meets the surface at 50° to the normal. Will it enter the air?
W: Critical angle for water → air: C ≈ 48.8°. | i = 50° > C. | So TIR occurs – no ray enters the air.
T: Bigger than C → reflected back.
@phys11-u1-q76
W: sin C = n₂/n₁ (from the denser to the less dense). | sin C = 1.33/1.52 = 0.875. | C = sin⁻¹(0.875) ≈ 61.0°.
T: The closer the two n values, the larger C.
@phys11-u1-q77
W: i = 0°: the ray travels along the normal. | Snell's law gives r = 0°, so there is no bending. | The light slows (v = c/1.33) and its wavelength shortens, but its direction stays the same.
T: Frequency never changes on refraction.
@phys11-u1-q78
W: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u. | 1/v = 1/12 − 1/36 = 3/36 − 1/36 = 2/36 = 1/18. | v = 18 cm – real, on the far side.
T: Object beyond 2F (24 cm) → image between F and 2F.
@phys11-u1-q79
Q: Using the previous question (f = 12 cm, u = 36 cm, v = 18 cm), find the magnification and the nature of the image.
W: |m| = v/u. | |m| = 18/36 = 0.5. | Real, inverted, half-size.
T: Real images from a single lens are always inverted.
@phys11-u1-q80
W: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/12 − 1/8. | = 2/24 − 3/24 = −1/24, so v = −24 cm. | Virtual, upright, magnified (m = 24/8 = 3) – the magnifying-glass case.
T: u < f → virtual image.
@phys11-u1-q81
W: A diverging lens has negative f: f = −25 cm = −0.25 m. | P = 1/f = 1/(−0.25 m). | P = −4.0 D.
T: Diverging = negative power.
@phys11-u1-q82
W: 1/f = 1/u + 1/v = 1/20 + 1/20. | = 2/20 = 1/10. | f = 10 cm; the object is at 2f = 20 cm and the real image is also at 2f.
T: Equal object and image distances → both at 2F.
@phys11-u1-q83
Q: A glass has n = √2. Find the critical angle from glass to air.
W: sin C = 1/n. | sin C = 1/√2 = 0.707. | C = 45°.
T: n = √2 is chosen so that C is exactly 45° (used in prism periscopes).
@phys11-u1-q84
W: c = fλ, so λ = c/f. | λ = (3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz). | λ = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m = 500 nm.
T: 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.
@phys11-u1-q85
W: A magnifying glass needs a virtual, enlarged, upright image. | A convex lens gives this only when the object is closer than the focal length. | So the object is placed between the lens and F.
T: Hold the lens close to the object.
@phys11-u1-q86
W: Part A (refraction): sin r = sin 30°/1.5 = 0.333, so r ≈ 19.5° inside the glass. | Part B (lens, real-is-positive): 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/10 − 1/15 = 3/30 − 2/30 = 1/30. | v = 30 cm – a real image 30 cm from the lens.
T: Split a combined problem into parts and use one formula per part.
@phys11-u1-q87
W: A denser medium has a larger n, so light slows down. | Snell's law: sin r = (n₁/n₂) sin i < sin i, so r < i. | The ray bends toward the normal – answer B.
T: Rarer → denser: toward; denser → rarer: away.
@phys11-u2-q01
W: Like poles repel; unlike poles attract. | Two N poles are like poles. | So they repel.
T: Repulsion is the only sure test that an object is a magnet.
@phys11-u2-q02
W: Unlike poles attract. | N and S are unlike. | So they attract each other.
T: Like repel, unlike attract.
@phys11-u2-q03
W: A magnet is made of tiny aligned magnetic domains. | Cutting it leaves the domains aligned in each piece. | So each half becomes a complete magnet with its own N and S poles.
T: You can never get a single isolated pole.
@phys11-u2-q05
W: Soft iron is easily magnetised but loses its magnetism as soon as the magnetising field is removed (temporary). | Steel is harder to magnetise but keeps its magnetism (permanent). | So soft iron is used for electromagnet and transformer cores; steel for permanent magnets.
T: Soft iron: easy come, easy go. Steel: hard come, hard go.
@phys11-u2-q06
W: Ferromagnetic materials are strongly attracted by magnets and can be magnetised. | The three elements are iron, nickel and cobalt. | Many alloys of them (e.g. steel) are ferromagnetic too.
T: Remember "I Nick Coins": Iron, Nickel, Cobalt.
@phys11-u2-q07
W: A compass needle is a small magnet that lines up with the magnetic field at its position. | Near a strong magnet, the magnet's field is far stronger than the Earth's. | So the needle points along the magnet's field lines, not to geographic north.
T: Use compasses to plot field lines.
@phys11-u2-q08
W: Around a magnet, both the magnet's field and the Earth's field act. | At some points they are equal in size and opposite in direction. | There the resultant field is zero – a neutral point (a compass shows no preferred direction).
T: Neutral point: B_magnet = B_Earth, opposite directions.
@phys11-u2-q09
W: The field of a coil grows with the current I and the number of turns N. | A soft-iron core concentrates the field lines and multiplies the field. | So: increase the current, add turns, and use a soft-iron core.
T: More current, more turns, iron core.
@phys11-u2-q10
W: The current creates the magnetism. | Soft iron does not keep magnetism when the current stops. | So the field almost disappears – which is why scrap-yard cranes can drop their load.
T: Steel cores would stay magnetised – bad for an electromagnet.
@phys11-u2-q11
W: Faraday's law: an emf is induced whenever the magnetic flux linking a circuit changes. | The size of the emf equals the rate of change of flux linkage: ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt. | No change in flux → no emf.
T: Change is the key word.
@phys11-u2-q12
W: Flux measures how much field passes through an area. | For a uniform field: Φ = BA cos θ (θ between B and the normal to the area). | Unit: weber (Wb) = T·m².
T: Face-on (θ = 0) gives the maximum flux BA.
@phys11-u2-q13
W: ε = N ΔΦ/Δt. | Doubling N with the same rate of change of flux doubles ε. | So the induced emf doubles.
T: More turns = more emf (each turn adds its own emf).
@phys11-u2-q15
W: The induced current flows in a direction that creates its own magnetic field. | That field opposes the change in flux that caused it. | This follows from conservation of energy.
T: Lenz: the induced effect fights the change.
@phys11-u2-q17
W: An emf needs a changing flux (ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt). | A magnet resting in a coil gives a constant flux: ΔΦ = 0. | So ε = 0 – there must be relative motion or a changing field.
T: Still magnet = no emf.
@phys11-u2-q18
W: Transformer equation: Vs/Vp = Ns/Np. | Vs = Vp × Ns/Np = 240 V × 100/1200. | Vs = 20 V – a step-down transformer.
T: Fewer secondary turns → lower voltage.
@phys11-u2-q19
W: An ideal transformer has no energy losses. | Power in = power out. | So IpVp = IsVs: if the voltage is stepped up, the current is stepped down.
T: Volts × amps stays the same (ideal).
@phys11-u2-q20
W: The changing flux also induces currents in the iron core itself (eddy currents). | These heat the core and waste energy. | Thin insulated laminations break up the current paths, so eddy currents and heating are reduced.
T: Laminate to limit eddy currents.
@phys11-u2-q21
W: The secondary emf needs a changing flux. | AC reverses continually, so the flux it produces keeps changing. | Steady DC gives a constant flux – no emf in the secondary after switching on.
T: Transformers work only with AC.
@phys11-u2-q22
W: A changing current in coil A makes a changing magnetic flux. | Part of that flux passes through nearby coil B. | The changing flux induces an emf in B – mutual induction (the transformer principle).
T: Mutual = between two coils.
@phys11-u2-q23
W: When the current in a coil changes, its own magnetic flux changes. | This changing flux induces an emf in the same coil. | By Lenz's law the emf opposes the change of current – a back emf.
T: Self = the coil acts on itself.
@phys11-u2-q25
W: The Earth behaves like a giant bar magnet. | Its magnetic south pole lies near the geographic North Pole. | The compass N pole is attracted to that magnetic S pole, so it points roughly north.
T: Unlike poles attract – the Earth's "north" is a magnetic south pole.
@phys11-u2-q27
W: Current flows through the electromagnet, which attracts the iron armature so the hammer strikes the gong. | Moving the armature opens the contact, so the current stops and the electromagnet switches off. | The spring pulls the armature back, the contact closes again and the cycle repeats – make-and-break.
T: Make-and-break = the circuit switches itself on and off rapidly.
@phys11-u2-q28
W: A coil is rotated in a magnetic field (or a magnet is rotated in a coil). | The flux through the coil changes continually, so an emf is induced (Faraday). | Slip rings give AC output; a split-ring commutator gives DC.
T: Generator: motion in → electricity out.
@phys11-u2-q29
W: Motor: a current in a magnetic field feels a force, so electrical energy → motion. | Generator: motion of a conductor in a field induces a current, so motion → electrical energy. | They have the same parts (coil, magnet) used in reverse.
T: Motor uses current; generator makes current.
@phys11-u2-q30
W: A step-up transformer has more secondary than primary turns: Ns > Np, so Vs > Vp. | Ideal: IpVp = IsVs, so Is < Ip. | Power stations step up to high voltage so the current in the lines is small and I²R losses are small.
T: High voltage, low current, low loss.
@phys11-u2-q31
Q: Two like magnetic poles are brought together. What force acts?
W: Like poles (N–N or S–S) repel. | Unlike poles (N–S) attract. | So like poles push apart.
T: Like repel, unlike attract.
@phys11-u2-q33
Q: A bar magnet is sawn in half. What poles does each piece have?
W: Magnetism comes from aligned domains throughout the magnet. | Each piece still has aligned domains. | So each piece is a complete magnet with N and S poles.
T: No single (isolated) magnetic poles exist.
@phys11-u2-q34
W: Field lines are drawn so that their spacing shows field strength. | Closely packed lines mean many lines through a small area. | So the field is stronger there (e.g. near the poles).
T: Crowded lines = strong field.
@phys11-u2-q35
Q: Where do magnetic field lines start and end?
W: Outside the magnet, lines leave the N pole and enter the S pole. | Inside the magnet they continue from S back to N. | So magnetic field lines form closed loops – they have no start or end.
T: Unlike electric field lines, magnetic lines are closed loops.
@phys11-u2-q36
W: A field line shows the direction a compass N pole would point. | At any point the field has only one direction. | Crossing lines would mean two directions at one point, so they never cross.
T: One point, one field direction.
@phys11-u2-q37
W: Ferromagnetic metals are strongly magnetic. | The elements are iron, nickel and cobalt. | Steel (mostly iron) is also ferromagnetic.
T: Copper, aluminium and brass are not magnetic.
@phys11-u2-q38
W: Soft iron is magnetised only while the current produces a field. | When the current stops, its domains become random again. | So it loses most of its magnetism – it is a temporary magnet.
T: That is why soft iron is used for electromagnet cores.
@phys11-u2-q39
W: Stroking aligns the domains of the steel in one direction. | Steel holds its domains in place after the magnet is removed. | So the steel bar becomes a permanent magnet.
T: Stroke in one direction only, lifting the magnet away each time.
@phys11-u2-q40
W: B of a coil ∝ current × turns per length. | A soft-iron core greatly increases the field. | So increase I, add turns, use a soft-iron core and keep the air gap short.
T: The same list as for a stronger solenoid.
@phys11-u2-q42
W: Between two like poles, the two fields point in opposite directions. | At one point (midway for equal magnets) they are equal in size. | They cancel there, giving a neutral point where B = 0.
T: Neutral points appear between LIKE poles, not unlike ones.
@phys11-u2-q43
Q: The Earth's field is roughly horizontal at the equator. What is the angle of dip there?
W: Angle of dip = angle between the Earth's field and the horizontal. | At the equator the field is horizontal, so dip ≈ 0°. | Dip increases toward the poles, reaching about 90° at the magnetic poles.
T: Dip: 0° at the equator, 90° at the poles.
@phys11-u2-q44
Q: Right-hand grip rule for a solenoid: if the fingers curl in the direction of the current, what does the thumb show?
W: Grip the solenoid with the right hand, fingers following the current round the turns. | The thumb points along the coil's axis. | It points to the north pole of the solenoid.
T: Check: looking at an end, anticlockwise current = N pole.
@phys11-u2-q45
Q: The current in a solenoid is increased. What happens to its magnetic field B?
W: B inside a solenoid is proportional to the current (B = µ₀nI). | Increasing I increases B. | With an iron core the field rises until the core saturates.
T: Double I → double B (no saturation).
@phys11-u2-q46
W: When the current stops, the electromagnet releases the armature. | The spring pulls the armature back to the contact screw. | This closes the circuit again so the cycle repeats.
T: Without the spring the bell would strike only once.
@phys11-u2-q47
W: Convert the area: 50 cm² = 50 × 10⁻⁴ m² = 5.0 × 10⁻³ m². | Face-on: Φ = BA = 0.20 T × 5.0 × 10⁻³ m². | Φ = 1.0 × 10⁻³ Wb.
T: 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m².
@phys11-u2-q48
W: Φ = BA cos θ, with θ = 30° between B and the normal. | Φ = 0.20 × 5.0 × 10⁻³ × cos 30° = 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.866. | Φ ≈ 8.7 × 10⁻⁴ Wb.
T: θ is measured from the normal, not from the surface.
@phys11-u2-q50
W: ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt. | The minus sign shows the direction: the induced emf drives a current whose field opposes the change in flux. | That is Lenz's law, which follows from conservation of energy.
T: For the size of ε, use |ε| = N ΔΦ/Δt.
@phys11-u2-q52
Q: A magnet sits still inside a coil. What emf is induced?
W: ε = N ΔΦ/Δt. | The magnet is still, so the flux is constant: ΔΦ = 0. | ε = 0.
T: Induction needs change.
@phys11-u2-q53
Q: A coil rotates at steady speed in a uniform magnetic field. What emf is produced?
W: As the coil turns, the flux through it changes continuously (Φ = BA cos ωt). | The flux rises and falls, so the induced emf keeps reversing. | The result is an alternating emf – the principle of the AC generator.
T: The emf is largest when the coil's plane is parallel to the field.
@phys11-u2-q55
Q: A motor uses a current in a magnetic field to produce motion. What does a generator do?
W: A generator is a motor in reverse. | Moving a conductor in a magnetic field changes the flux. | This induces a current – motion produces electricity.
T: Motor: electrical → kinetic. Generator: kinetic → electrical.
@phys11-u2-q56
Q: A transformer has Vp = 110 V, Np = 440 and Ns = 40. Find Vs.
W: Vs/Vp = Ns/Np. | Vs = 110 V × 40/440. | Vs = 10 V.
T: Turns ratio 440:40 = 11:1 → voltage ÷ 11.
@phys11-u2-q57
W: Ideal transformer: IpVp = IsVs. | Ip = IsVs/Vp = (3.0 A × 10 V) ÷ 110 V = 30/110. | Ip ≈ 0.27 A.
T: Power out = 30 W = power in.
@phys11-u2-q58
Q: A transformer has Ns = 2Np. Is it step-up or step-down, and how do the currents compare (ideal)?
W: Vs/Vp = Ns/Np = 2, so the voltage doubles – step-up. | Ideal: IpVp = IsVs. | So Is = Ip/2 – the current halves.
T: Voltage up ↔ current down.
@phys11-u2-q59
W: The changing flux induces eddy currents in a solid core. | These currents heat the core and waste energy. | Thin insulated laminations break the current loops, so less heat is produced.
T: Laminations are insulated from each other.
@phys11-u2-q60
W: Two coils are placed close together. | A changing current in coil A produces a changing flux through coil B. | This induces an emf in B – mutual induction.
T: Transformers work by mutual induction.
@phys11-u2-q61
W: A changing current in a coil changes the flux through that same coil. | So an emf is induced in the coil itself. | It opposes the change in current (back emf).
T: Self-induction slows down changes in current.
@phys11-u2-q62
W: Switching off makes the current fall to zero very quickly (large ΔI/Δt). | The self-induced emf is proportional to the rate of change of current. | So a very large emf appears across the switch and makes a spark.
T: Fast change → big emf.
@phys11-u2-q63
W: Steady DC gives a constant magnetic flux in the core. | ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt with ΔΦ = 0. | So the secondary emf is zero.
T: Only a changing primary current induces a secondary emf.
@phys11-u2-q65
Q: A coil's area is doubled while B and the motion stay the same. What happens to the induced emf?
W: Φ = BA, so doubling A doubles the flux. | With the same motion, the rate of change of flux also doubles. | So ε = N ΔΦ/Δt doubles.
T: ε ∝ N, B, A and speed of change.
@phys11-u2-q67
W: Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised. | It concentrates the flux and guides it from the primary to the secondary coil. | So almost all the flux from the primary links the secondary.
T: Soft iron also has low hysteresis losses.
@phys11-u2-q68
W: Break a magnet into pieces. | Every piece, however small, has both an N and an S pole. | So a single isolated N pole cannot be produced.
T: Magnetic poles always come in pairs.
@phys11-u2-q69
W: A magnet attracts any unmagnetised iron object by inducing magnetism in it. | So attraction does not prove the object is a magnet. | Only two magnets can repel, so repulsion is the sure test.
T: Test: bring a known pole near both ends.
@phys11-u2-q70
Q: An electromagnetic crane lifts scrap iron and then drops it. How is the circuit used?
W: Current ON: the soft-iron core becomes a strong magnet and lifts the scrap. | Current OFF: the soft iron loses its magnetism. | The scrap falls where the operator wants it.
T: Soft iron is the key to dropping the load.
@phys11-u2-q73
W: The crane must be strongly magnetic while lifting. | It must lose its magnetism when the current is switched off. | Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised, so it does both.
T: Steel would keep holding the scrap.
@phys11-u2-q74
W: Convert: 20 cm² = 20 × 10⁻⁴ m² = 2.0 × 10⁻³ m². | Φ = BA = 0.40 T × 2.0 × 10⁻³ m². | Φ = 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ Wb.
T: Area must be in m² to get webers.
@phys11-u2-q75
W: Φ = BA cos θ with θ = 60°. | Φ = 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ × cos 60° = 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.5. | Φ = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ Wb.
T: At 90° (field along the surface) the flux is zero.
@phys11-u2-q76
Q: The flux through a 200-turn coil falls from 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ Wb to 0 in 0.10 s. Find the size of the induced emf.
W: |ε| = N ΔΦ/Δt. | |ε| = 200 × (4.0 × 10⁻⁴ Wb) ÷ 0.10 s. | |ε| = 0.80 V.
T: ΔΦ = final − initial; use its size for |ε|.
@phys11-u2-q77
Q: A magnet is pushed N-pole first into a coil. What pole is induced on the coil face nearest the magnet?
W: Lenz's law: the induced current opposes the change (the approaching N pole). | To oppose it, the coil face must repel the magnet. | So that face becomes a N pole.
T: Approaching → repel; leaving → attract.
@phys11-u2-q78
Q: A transformer has a 240 V primary with 800 turns and a 40-turn secondary. Find Vs.
W: Vs = Vp × Ns/Np. | Vs = 240 V × 40/800. | Vs = 12 V.
T: 800:40 = 20:1 → 240 ÷ 20 = 12 V.
@phys11-u2-q79
W: Ideal: IpVp = IsVs. | Ip = IsVs/Vp = 2.0 A × 12 V ÷ 240 V. | Ip = 0.10 A.
T: Power = 24 W on both sides.
@phys11-u2-q80
Q: Why can't a transformer work on DC?
W: Steady DC produces a constant flux in the core. | After the instant of switching on, ΔΦ = 0. | So no emf is induced in the secondary (ε = 0).
T: Transformers need a changing (AC) current.
@phys11-u2-q81
Q: A transformer steps up 11 kV to 132 kV. Find the turns ratio Ns/Np.
W: Ns/Np = Vs/Vp. | = 132 kV ÷ 11 kV. | = 12 – the secondary has twelve times as many turns.
T: kV cancel – no need to convert.
@phys11-u2-q83
W: Step-down means Vs < Vp. | Vs/Vp = Ns/Np, so Ns < Np. | Iron cores are used (C wrong) and transformers need AC (D wrong) – answer B.
T: Step-down: fewer secondary turns.
@phys11-u3-q05
Q: The frequency of a sound is doubled in the same air. What happens to its wavelength?
W: v = fλ, and the speed of sound in the same air stays the same. | λ = v/f, so doubling f halves λ. | The wavelength halves.
T: Same medium → same speed.
@phys11-u3-q06
W: Amplitude decides how much energy the wave carries – the loudness. | Pitch depends only on frequency, which is unchanged. | So the sound is louder with the same pitch.
T: Amplitude ↔ loudness; frequency ↔ pitch.
@phys11-u3-q07
W: In a transverse wave the particles vibrate at right angles to the direction the wave travels. | A wave on a rope moves along while the rope moves up and down. | Light and all EM waves are also transverse.
T: Transverse = across.
@phys11-u3-q08
W: In a longitudinal wave the particles vibrate along the direction of travel. | Sound in air travels as compressions and rarefactions. | So sound in air is longitudinal (also a slinky pushed back and forth).
T: Longitudinal = along.
@phys11-u3-q09
W: Sound is a mechanical wave – particles pass the vibration on. | A vacuum has no particles. | So sound cannot travel through a vacuum.
T: Bell-jar experiment: the ringing fades as air is pumped out.
@phys11-u3-q10
W: Light is an electromagnetic wave – oscillating electric and magnetic fields. | Fields do not need particles. | So light travels through a vacuum (sunlight crosses space).
T: EM waves need no medium.
@phys11-u3-q11
W: The sound travels to the cliff and back: total distance 2d = vt. | 2d = 340 m/s × 2.0 s = 680 m. | d = 340 m.
T: Echo: always halve the total distance.
@phys11-u3-q12
W: d = vt/2. | d = 340 m/s × 0.6 s ÷ 2. | d = 102 m.
T: The time is for the round trip.
@phys11-u3-q13
W: Sound passes on by particles pushing their neighbours. | In steel the particles are closer and more strongly bonded (stiffer). | So the vibration passes along faster (about 5000 m/s vs 340 m/s in air).
T: Speed of sound: solids > liquids > gases.
@phys11-u3-q14
W: In warm air the molecules move faster. | They pass the vibration on more quickly. | So sound travels a little faster in warm air (about 0.6 m/s per °C).
T: Warm air → slightly faster sound.
@phys11-u3-q15
W: A wavelength is the distance between two neighbouring points in the same phase. | Two neighbouring crests are in the same phase. | So crest to next crest = one wavelength λ.
T: Trough to next trough is also λ.
@phys11-u3-q16
W: In a longitudinal wave, compressions play the role of crests. | Neighbouring compressions are in the same phase. | So compression to next compression = one wavelength λ.
T: Compression to the next rarefaction = λ/2.
@phys11-u3-q17
W: The source vibrates a certain number of times per second. | The wave carries that same number of vibrations per second into any medium. | So frequency is set by the source; speed and wavelength depend on the medium.
T: Crossing into a new medium, f stays the same.
@phys11-u3-q18
Q: A radio wave has λ = 300 m (c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s). Find its frequency.
W: c = fλ, so f = c/λ. | f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ 300 m. | f = 1 × 10⁶ Hz = 1 MHz.
T: Long wavelength → low frequency.
@phys11-u3-q19
Q: Which has the higher frequency, yellow light or red light?
W: All light travels at c in a vacuum, so f = c/λ. | Yellow light has a shorter wavelength than red. | So yellow has the higher frequency.
T: Red has the longest wavelength and lowest frequency of visible light.
@phys11-u3-q20
W: The EM spectrum is ordered by frequency (or wavelength). | Lowest f: radio waves, then microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays. | Highest f: gamma rays.
T: Mnemonic: "Raging Martians Invaded Venus Using X-ray Guns".
@phys11-u3-q21
W: Microwaves are absorbed by water molecules in food, making them vibrate and heat up. | Microwaves also pass through the atmosphere and carry signals. | So they are used for cooking and mobile phones.
T: Microwave ovens and phones use similar frequencies (about 1–3 GHz).
@phys11-u3-q22
W: X-rays pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by dense bone. | A detector behind the body shows bones as light shadows. | So X-rays are used to image bones.
T: X-rays are ionising – limit exposure.
@phys11-u3-q23
W: UV has a high frequency, so each photon carries a lot of energy. | It can damage skin cells (sunburn, skin cancer) and eyes. | The ozone layer absorbs much of the Sun's UV.
T: Higher frequency EM = more dangerous.
@phys11-u3-q24
W: All EM waves are oscillating electric and magnetic fields. | In a vacuum they all travel at the same speed. | c ≈ 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.
T: Only f and λ differ between EM waves.
@phys11-u3-q25
Q: Do the particles of the medium travel along with the wave?
W: Each particle vibrates about a fixed position. | The vibration (energy) is passed on from particle to particle. | So energy travels, but the particles do not move along with the wave.
T: A floating cork bobs up and down but does not travel with the wave.
@phys11-u3-q26
W: Points one wavelength apart are at the same stage of their vibration. | They move up and down together. | So they are in phase.
T: In phase: separation = λ, 2λ, 3λ …
@phys11-u3-q27
W: Points half a wavelength apart are at opposite stages. | When one is at a crest, the other is at a trough. | So they are in opposite phase (antiphase).
T: Antiphase: separation = λ/2, 3λ/2, 5λ/2 …
@phys11-u3-q28
W: Intensity = energy arriving per second per unit area. | Intensity grows with the square of the amplitude. | Greater intensity is heard as a louder sound.
T: Loudness ↔ amplitude/intensity.
@phys11-u3-q29
W: Pitch is how high or low a note sounds. | It depends on how many vibrations reach the ear each second. | Higher frequency → higher pitch.
T: Human hearing: about 20 Hz to 20 kHz.
@phys11-u3-q30
W: When a wave hits a barrier and bounces back, it is reflected. | The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection (measured from the normal). | Its speed, frequency and wavelength stay the same.
T: All waves obey the same law of reflection as light.
@phys11-u3-q36
W: v depends only on the medium (air), so it stays the same. | v = fλ, so if f doubles, λ must halve. | v unchanged, λ halves.
T: Change f in the same medium: λ changes, v does not.
@phys11-u3-q37
Q: The amplitude of a wave is tripled. Roughly what happens to its energy?
W: Energy carried by a wave ∝ amplitude². | (3)² = 9. | The energy becomes about 9 times larger; the pitch (frequency) stays the same.
T: Energy ∝ A².
@phys11-u3-q38
W: The frequency is set by the source (440 Hz), so it is the same in both. | Sound travels faster in water (≈ 1500 m/s) than in air (≈ 340 m/s). | λ = v/f, so the wavelength is larger in water.
T: f fixed, v and λ change together.
@phys11-u3-q39
W: Transverse: particles vibrate perpendicular (⊥) to the direction of travel. | Longitudinal: particles vibrate parallel (∥) to the direction of travel. | Examples: light (transverse), sound (longitudinal).
T: T for Transverse and "T-shape" (at right angles).
@phys11-u3-q40
Q: What type of wave is sound in air?
W: Air molecules vibrate back and forth along the direction the sound travels. | This makes compressions (high pressure) and rarefactions (low pressure). | So sound in air is longitudinal.
T: Gases cannot carry transverse waves.
@phys11-u3-q41
Q: What type of wave is light?
W: Light consists of electric and magnetic fields oscillating. | The fields oscillate at right angles to the direction of travel. | So light is a transverse electromagnetic wave.
T: Polarisation proves light is transverse.
@phys11-u3-q42
W: Sound is a mechanical wave carried by vibrating particles, so it needs a medium. | Light is an EM wave made of fields, so it needs none. | Sound cannot cross a vacuum; light can.
T: In space, explosions are silent.
@phys11-u3-q43
W: The speed of sound in air depends on temperature. | At room temperature it is about 340 m/s (330 m/s at 0 °C). | In exams, use the value the question gives.
T: Sound is about a million times slower than light.
@phys11-u3-q44
Q: An echo returns after 0.40 s (v = 340 m/s). How far away is the reflector?
W: d = vt/2 (the sound travels there and back). | d = 340 m/s × 0.40 s ÷ 2. | d = 68 m.
T: Halve the round trip.
@phys11-u3-q45
Q: A reflector is 255 m away (v = 340 m/s). How long does the echo take?
W: The sound travels 2d: t = 2d/v. | t = 2 × 255 m ÷ 340 m/s = 510/340. | t = 1.50 s.
T: Distance doubles for an echo.
@phys11-u3-q46
Q: A bat's pulse returns after 0.020 s (v = 340 m/s). How far away is the insect?
W: d = vt/2. | d = 340 m/s × 0.020 s ÷ 2. | d = 3.4 m.
T: Bats use ultrasound echoes to find prey.
@phys11-u3-q47
Q: Why does sound travel faster in steel than in air?
W: Steel particles are closely packed and strongly bonded (high stiffness). | A disturbance is passed on very quickly from particle to particle. | In air the molecules are far apart, so the vibration passes more slowly.
T: Put your ear to a rail – the sound arrives before the sound in air.
@phys11-u3-q48
W: Sound speed in a gas increases with temperature. | Warmer molecules move faster and pass on the vibration sooner. | So sound is slightly faster in warm air.
T: About +0.6 m/s for each 1 °C rise.
@phys11-u3-q49
Q: What is the distance from one crest to the next-but-one crest?
W: Crest to the next crest = λ. | Next-but-one crest is two crests along. | Distance = 2λ.
T: Count the gaps between crests, not the crests.
@phys11-u3-q50
W: Compression to compression = λ. | A rarefaction lies halfway between two compressions. | So compression to adjacent rarefaction = λ/2.
T: Like crest to trough on a transverse wave.
@phys11-u3-q51
Q: Two points on a wave are one wavelength apart. What is their phase relation?
W: One wavelength apart means one whole cycle apart. | They are at the same stage of vibration. | So they are in phase.
T: Whole numbers of λ → in phase.
@phys11-u3-q52
Q: Two points on a wave are λ/2 apart. What is their phase relation?
W: λ/2 is half a cycle. | One point is at a crest when the other is at a trough. | They are in antiphase (opposite phase).
T: Odd multiples of λ/2 → antiphase.
@phys11-u3-q53
W: The wave's frequency equals the frequency of the vibrating source. | Changing the medium changes v and λ, not f. | So the source determines the frequency.
T: f is "born" at the source.
@phys11-u3-q54
W: Loudness depends on how much energy arrives per second. | That depends on the amplitude (intensity ∝ A²). | So loudness depends on amplitude.
T: Bigger amplitude = louder.
@phys11-u3-q55
W: Pitch is how high a note sounds. | It depends on the number of vibrations per second. | So pitch depends on frequency.
T: Higher f = higher pitch.
@phys11-u3-q56
Q: A radio wave has λ = 3.0 m. Find its frequency.
W: f = c/λ. | f = (3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ 3.0 m. | f = 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz = 100 MHz (FM radio).
T: 1 MHz = 10⁶ Hz.
@phys11-u3-q57
Q: A microwave has f = 2.45 GHz. Find its wavelength.
W: λ = c/f, with 2.45 GHz = 2.45 × 10⁹ Hz. | λ = (3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (2.45 × 10⁹ Hz). | λ ≈ 0.122 m (12.2 cm).
T: Convert GHz to Hz (× 10⁹) first.
@phys11-u3-q59
W: Wavelength increases from gamma toward radio. | Of the four: X-rays (shortest), then green light, then infrared. | Radio has the longest wavelength: X → green → IR → radio.
T: Short λ = high f = high energy.
@phys11-u3-q60
W: For EM waves f = c/λ, so the order of f is the reverse of the order of λ. | Radio has the lowest f, then IR, then green light. | X-rays have the highest: radio → IR → green → X.
T: Reverse the wavelength order to get the frequency order.
@phys11-u3-q62
Q: At what speed do all EM waves travel in a vacuum?
W: All EM waves are the same kind of wave – electric and magnetic fields. | In a vacuum they all travel at the same speed. | c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.
T: Radio and gamma rays both travel at c.
@phys11-u3-q63
Q: Water waves reflect off a straight wall. What law do they obey?
W: The wave hits the wall at an angle to the normal. | It bounces back at the same angle on the other side of the normal. | Law of reflection: angle of incidence = angle of reflection.
T: Use a ripple tank to see this.
@phys11-u3-q64
Q: Four waves pass a post in 2.0 s. What is the frequency?
W: Frequency = number of waves ÷ time. | f = 4 ÷ 2.0 s. | f = 2.0 Hz.
T: 1 Hz = 1 wave per second.
@phys11-u3-q65
Q: Those waves (f = 2.0 Hz) have λ = 1.2 m. What is their speed?
W: v = fλ. | v = 2.0 Hz × 1.2 m. | v = 2.4 m/s.
T: Hz × m = m/s.
@phys11-u3-q66
Q: A 6.0 m string holds exactly 3 full waves. What is the wavelength?
W: Length = number of waves × λ. | 6.0 m = 3λ. | λ = 2.0 m.
T: Count full waves (crest + trough) carefully.
@phys11-u3-q67
Q: That string wave (λ = 2.0 m) vibrates at 20 Hz. What is its speed?
W: v = fλ. | v = 20 Hz × 2.0 m. | v = 40 m/s.
T: Always write the formula before substituting.
@phys11-u3-q68
Q: Can a person hear a sound of 30 kHz?
W: The human hearing range is about 20 Hz to 20 kHz. | 30 kHz is above 20 kHz – ultrasound. | So people usually cannot hear it, but dogs and bats can.
T: Above 20 kHz = ultrasound; below 20 Hz = infrasound.
@phys11-u3-q69
Q: Can a 1 MHz radio wave be heard directly by the ear?
W: The ear detects mechanical vibrations of air (sound) between 20 Hz and 20 kHz. | A radio wave is an electromagnetic wave, not a vibration of air. | So it cannot be heard directly – a radio must convert it into sound.
T: Radio waves ≠ sound waves.
@phys11-u3-q70
W: A wave carries energy from the source to elsewhere. | The particles of the medium only vibrate about fixed positions. | So a wave transfers energy, not matter.
T: Energy moves, matter stays.
@phys11-u3-q71
W: Lenses in contact: P = P₁ + P₂ = +2.5 D + (−0.5 D). | P = +2.0 D. | f = 1/P = 1/2.0 = 0.50 m – the combination is converging.
T: Positive total power → acts as a convex lens.
@phys11-u3-q72
Q: A tuning fork has f = 512 Hz. What is the period?
W: T = 1/f. | T = 1 ÷ 512 Hz. | T ≈ 1.95 × 10⁻³ s (about 2 ms).
T: Period and frequency are reciprocals.
@phys11-u3-q73
Q: Sound travels at 340 m/s with f = 680 Hz. What is its wavelength?
W: λ = v/f. | λ = 340 m/s ÷ 680 Hz. | λ = 0.50 m.
T: Check: 680 × 0.5 = 340 ✓.
@phys11-u3-q74
W: The frequency stays 680 Hz (set by the source). | λ = v/f = 1500 m/s ÷ 680 Hz. | λ ≈ 2.21 m.
T: Faster medium → longer wavelength.
@phys11-u3-q75
Q: An echo from a wall returns in 0.50 s (v = 340 m/s). How far away is the wall?
W: d = vt/2. | d = 340 × 0.50 ÷ 2. | d = 85 m.
T: Echo time is the round trip.
@phys11-u3-q76
Q: You stand 170 m from a cliff (v = 340 m/s). How long until you hear the echo?
W: The sound travels 2 × 170 = 340 m. | t = 2d/v = 340 m ÷ 340 m/s. | t = 1.00 s.
T: t = 2d/v for echoes.
@phys11-u3-q77
W: f = c/λ. | f = (3.00 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ 150 m. | f = 2.00 × 10⁶ Hz = 2.00 MHz.
T: AM radio wavelengths are hundreds of metres.
@phys11-u3-q78
Q: The amplitude of a sound wave is doubled. What happens to its pitch?
W: Pitch depends only on frequency. | Doubling the amplitude does not change the frequency. | So the pitch is unchanged; the sound is louder (energy ×4).
T: Amplitude → loudness only.
@phys11-u3-q79
Q: Why can't you hear a hammer striking a rock in the distance on the Moon's surface?
W: The Moon has no atmosphere – near vacuum. | Sound is a mechanical (longitudinal) wave and needs a medium. | So no sound reaches you; you would still see the hammer because light (EM) crosses a vacuum.
T: Astronauts talk by radio.
@phys11-u3-q80
W: Frequency increases from radio to gamma. | Of these: radio (lowest), green light, then UV. | X-rays highest: radio → green → UV → X-rays.
T: Visible light sits between IR and UV.
@phys11-u3-q81
W: λ/2 apart → antiphase; λ apart → in phase. | 3λ/2 = λ + λ/2 – one full cycle plus half a cycle. | So the points are in opposite phase.
T: Odd multiples of λ/2 → antiphase.
@phys11-u3-q82
Q: Four crests of a string wave pass a point in 2.0 s. What is the frequency?
W: Each crest is one cycle passing the point. | f = 4 cycles ÷ 2.0 s. | f = 2.0 Hz.
T: f = number of cycles ÷ time.
@phys11-u3-q83
Q: Those crests (f = 2.0 Hz) are 0.80 m apart. What is the wave speed?
W: Crest-to-crest distance = λ = 0.80 m. | v = fλ = 2.0 Hz × 0.80 m. | v = 1.60 m/s.
T: Neighbouring crests are one wavelength apart.
@phys11-u3-q84
W: In the same medium v stays constant. | v = fλ, so λ = v/f. | Doubling f halves λ – answer C.
T: f and λ are inversely proportional at fixed v.
@phys11-u3-q85
W: Sound is a mechanical wave carried by particles. | A vacuum contains no particles. | So sound cannot travel in a vacuum – answer D.
T: Only EM waves travel through a vacuum.
